    "notice": "The author Safe's ownership history: who was an owner, and from which         block. Installed as a UUPS proxy per author Safe, one proxy per Safe,         exactly like DraftsModuleV1. Why this exists (the short version):   The gate on the publication module asks `Safe.isOwner(signer)` — present   tense. Rotate one owner and every earlier signature over the author's work   stops verifying, even though the work did not change. This module keeps the   missing tense: `isOwnerAt` and its timestamp twin `wasOwnerAt`. How it stays current:   Safe 1.4.1 has no owner-rotation callback. The only on-chain tap is the   transaction guard (`setGuard` is `onlySelf`, so a Safe tx installs it, and   `execTransaction` calls `checkAfterExecution` every time). Every owner   mutation is `onlySelf`, so every rotation passes through the guard, and the   guard — `OwnerHistoryGuard`, a separate contract because a revert there   would brick the Safe — calls `sync()` here.   `sync()` reads the Safe's live owner set and diffs it against the current   era. It is permissionless and a pure function of chain state: the only   thing it can write is what the Safe already says. That is deliberate — it   is also the repair path when the hook is missed (a module enable/disable   window, a guard removed for a Safe upgrade, an out-of-gas hook).   The tap is not a guarantee, and the guard is not the primary path:     - The guard is **best-effort**. A Safe transaction whose gas limit is the       estimated cost starves the hook, and the era is then stamped at the next       `sync()`. Rotations proposed by this project's tooling should carry an       explicit `sync()` call; the guard catches the ones that do not.     - `execTransactionFromModule` is a self-call and skips guards, so an       enabled Safe module can rotate owners and can `setGuard(address(0))`       with no hook at all. Guard coverage is over *transactions*, not over a       malicious module. Again: `sync()` repairs it. The half-open era model:   Era k covers blocks `[startBlock, endBlock)`; `endBlock == 0` means \"still   current\". For a block strictly in the past the eras and the tenure index   are the whole answer, and at the boundary block — the block the Safe rotated   in — the new set is authoritative: the tenure of whoever left ends there and   the tenure of whoever joined starts there, so `isOwnerAt(x, boundary)` and   `isOwnerAt(y, boundary)` give exactly one true answer for the pair that   swapped, and no block belongs to two eras.   At the *head* the index is deliberately not consulted (see `isOwnerAt`):   inside the current block the answer comes from the Safe's live `isOwner`, so   it reflects whatever the pre-state or post-state of that block is at the   moment of the call. A gate judging the current block must therefore read   `isOwnerAt(who, block.number)` and accept that a rotation landing in the same   block may or may not be visible — wait a block before trusting it. Storage declaration order is the upgrade contract, same rule as the drafts module (INV-3 in the publication spec): `safe`, `eras`, `tenureBlocks`, `tenureTimes`, `ownersEverSet`, `seen`.",
